92186452448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3935095-5efb-57a9-a398-831d61d53049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452447} :answer-field/id #uuid "78acad20-e495-541a-94b6-43117568da9f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1b3df2e-4878-5f07-9682-4c09eb922bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-97945" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A+I)=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A+I)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A+I)\\ne 3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields 